<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Auto Backup, on API 30 and below. `data_extraction_rules.xml` is the same decision for
  API 31 and above; the platform reads one or the other by version, never both, so a rule
  added to one file and not the other is a rule that applies to half the install base.

  The auth-token store is excluded because backing it up cannot work and does not fail
  loudly. What Auto Backup would copy is the ciphertext; what it cannot copy is the
  AES key behind it, which lives in the Android keystore and is not part of the app's
  data by design. Restored onto a new device the file is intact, well-formed, and
  permanently undecryptable — the reader is signed out with no error anywhere, and the
  store has to be recognised as unrecoverable and cleared before they can sign in again
  (see `StoredTokenState.Discard`). Excluding it makes a restore produce a clean
  signed-out install instead, which is the honest outcome.

  The path is the file `preferencesDataStore(name = AUTH_TOKEN_STORE)` writes, relative to
  the app's `filesDir`. `TokenStorageContractTest` checks it against that constant, because
  this is a string in an XML file that nothing else would notice going stale.

  Excludes only, so everything not named here is still backed up.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/auth_tokens.preferences_pb" />
</full-backup-content>
